Instead of splitting first, this scans from the end of the string. A word is collected until a space appears, then it is placed into the result. Since the scan starts on the right, the words arrive in the needed order.

Dry run with `"cat  runs fast"`:

- Start at the end and collect `fast`.
- Skip the two spaces and collect `runs`.
- Collect `cat`.
- Join the pieces with one space.

```javascript
class Solution {
    reverseWords(s) {
        const words = [];
        let i = s.length - 1;

        while (i >= 0) {
            while (i >= 0 && s[i] === ' ') {
                i--;
            }

            if (i < 0) {
                break;
            }

            const end = i;
            while (i >= 0 && s[i] !== ' ') {
                i--;
            }

            words.push(s.slice(i + 1, end + 1));
        }

        return words.join(' ');
    }
}
```

Only one array of words is kept, and `join()` makes sure there is no extra space at either end. The input string stays unchanged.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the collected words.

This version keeps the work inside the input string. First, it removes extra spaces while copying each word forward. Then it reverses the whole cleaned string and reverses each word again. The two reversals put the words in reverse order while keeping each word readable.

A small dry run for `"the sky"` is:

- Cleaned text: `"the sky"`
- Reverse all characters: `"yks eht"`
- Reverse each word: `"sky the"`

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string reverseWords(string s) {
        int write = 0;
        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());

        for (int read = 0; read < n; read++) {
            if (s[read] == ' ') {
                continue;
            }

            if (write > 0) {
                s[write++] = ' ';
            }

            while (read < n && s[read] != ' ') {
                s[write++] = s[read++];
            }
        }

        s.resize(write);
        reverse(s.begin(), s.end());

        int start = 0;
        for (int end = 0; end <= write; end++) {
            if (end == write || s[end] == ' ') {
                reverse(s.begin() + start, s.begin() + end);
                start = end + 1;
            }
        }

        return s;
    }
};
```

The string parameter is passed by value, so this changes the local copy rather than the caller's original string. Leading, trailing, and repeated spaces are removed during the first pass.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the copied input string.

A stack fits this problem naturally because the last word needs to come out first. The code reads each word, pushes it onto a stack, and then removes the words in last-in-first-out order.

Repeated spaces are skipped because a word is pushed only when the `StringBuilder` has characters in it.

```java
class Solution {
    public String reverseWords(String s) {
        java.util.Deque<String> words = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        StringBuilder current = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char ch = s.charAt(i);

            if (ch == ' ') {
                if (current.length() > 0) {
                    words.push(current.toString());
                    current.setLength(0);
                }
            } else {
                current.append(ch);
            }
        }

        if (current.length() > 0) {
            words.push(current.toString());
        }

        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
        while (!words.isEmpty()) {
            if (result.length() > 0) {
                result.append(' ');
            }
            result.append(words.pop());
        }

        return result.toString();
    }
}
```

The empty string and a string containing only spaces return an empty result. This is easy to follow, but the stack stores every word.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

## Intuition

We need to reverse the **order of words** in the string.
Spaces can be extra (leading/trailing/multiple), but in the output we want **single spaces** between words and **no extra spaces** at ends.

So we scan the string from the **end**, pick one word at a time, and build the answer.

---

## Approach

1. Start from the last character of the string (`i = n-1`).
2. Skip all trailing spaces.
3. Mark the end of a word (`j = i`).
4. Move `i` left until we reach a space → now word is from `i+1` to `j`.
5. Add that word to the result.
6. Add a space **only if** result already has a word (to avoid trailing/leading spaces).
7. Repeat until `i < 0`.

---

## Solution

```java[]
class Solution {
    public String reverseWords(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        StringBuilder sentence = new StringBuilder();

        int i = n - 1;

        while (i >= 0) {
            // 1) skip spaces
            while (i >= 0 && s.charAt(i) == ' ') i--;
            if (i < 0) break;

            // 2) add space between words (not before first word)
            if (sentence.length() > 0) sentence.append(' ');

            // 3) find the current word boundaries
            int j = i;
            while (i >= 0 && s.charAt(i) != ' ') i--;

            // 4) append the word
            sentence.append(s.substring(i + 1, j + 1));
        }

        return sentence.toString();
    }
}
```

---

## Complexity

**Time Complexity:** `O(n)`
Each character is visited at most once while moving `i` from right to left.

**Space Complexity:** `O(n)`
We build the output string in `StringBuilder` (and `substring` creates word strings internally).
