The key observation is that we don't need to reverse the characters of each word. We only need to reverse the order of the words.

So, we can first separate the string into individual words and then traverse the words from right to left.

Using split("\\s+") also handles multiple spaces between words, while trim() removes leading and trailing spaces.

## Approach
1. Remove leading and trailing spaces using trim().
2. Split the string into words using:
split("\\s+")
3. This treats one or more spaces as a separator.
4. Traverse the resulting array from the last word to the first.
5. Append each word to a StringBuilder.
6. Add a single space between words to ensure there are no extra spaces in the result.
7. Return the constructed string.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public String reverseWords(String s) {
        String[] split = s.trim().split("\\s+");
		StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

		for(int i = split.length-1; i >= 0; i--) {
			sb = sb.append(split[i]);
			if (i != 0) {
                sb.append(" ");
            }
		}
		return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(n) — trim(), split(), and traversing the words together take linear time with respect to the length of the input string.

**Space Complexity:**
